[38;5;231m⏺[39m This is a bounded change: one existing page gets a new control. So I'll ask a few questions, give you a short design
  here in chat, and wait for your yes before writing any code. There's no spec file or plan doc for this.

  I've read NOTES.md and all four UI guideline files (01–04). Nothing has changed in the repo.

  The first question is where the control goes, which is easier to show than describe. I've opened a browser tab with
  four wireframes:

  [94m]8;id=isf0xq;http://localhost:58438/?key=35ecb3817713e847eb9ee6a09abc00a991469d2b22f60610b6395c4218fce602\http://localhost:58438/?key=35ecb3817713e847eb9ee6a09abc00a991469d2b22f60610b6395c4218fce602[39m]8;;\

  The browser tab uses a lot of tokens, so tell me if you'd rather keep this in the terminal.

  The four options:
  - [1mA. Filter bar above the table:[0m an Event select, a Search box and a result count. Controls are always visible and the
    layout wraps cleanly at 320px.
  - [1mB. A filter row inside the table header:[0m compact, but hard to label properly, and it breaks at 320px width.
  - [1mC. A left sidebar:[0m leaves room for more filters later, but squeezes the six-column table.
  - [1mD. A Filter button that opens a pop-up:[0m tidy, and applied filters show as removable tags. It takes an extra click to
    find failed sign-ins.

  [1mI recommend A.[0m The only support tickets so far came from people hunting for a failed sign-in they didn't recognize. A
  puts that filter on screen with no extra click, and it's the simplest to make meet the accessibility rules.

  One thing I'll need to ask about next: the guidelines require page CSS to use colour and spacing variables "declared
  in the shared token sheet". That sheet doesn't exist in this repo. No CSS file defines [38;5;153m--surface-panel[39m or any other
  token.

  Take a look and tell me which placement you prefer, here in the terminal. You can also click one in the browser.

